Plat Forming Plat

Pantheon design and Money Bin candidate · v0.1

A place to take one moment that could have gone another way, give someone room to play that possibility, and bring back what they made. Each branch can become a small platform of its own: a scene to continue, a decision to revisit, or an experiment to run.

The unit of exchange is a playable departure. Someone shows you exactly where they stepped off the original path and gives you enough context to follow theirs.

The first screen

|Left column                                                                  |Center                                                                                   |Right column                                                                                            |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|**The moment.** The original line and the context available when it happened.|**Your move.** Write a continuation, propose a real-world test, or leave the moment open.|**What came back.** Other continuations and observed outcomes, revealed after your move in blind replay.|

Every branch has three actions: Play it, Park it, and Bring something back. A branch that someone actually tries can return a result to its parent. The parent author can link that result into the main line, with credit and a visible record of the choice.

Three ways to play

|Mode                |What participants can do                                                                                             |What comes back                                                       |
|--------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|
|**Replay**          |Respond to the same source without seeing later replies.                                                             |Independent continuations from a documented starting point.           |
|**Change platforms**|Move the idea to a different audience, medium, time window, or measure of success.                                   |An account of what became possible and what the move cost.            |
|**Form a platform** |Give a branch its own miniature rules. Specify what people receive, what moves are open, and what counts as a return.|A reusable play surface somebody else can try with their own material.|

A source can travel from a social feed to a tasting table, then back as a report. The next group can fork the tasting format and measure something else. That is the recursive part: a branch can produce a new place to branch from.

The physics

1. Anchor. Every branch points to a specific version and moment. Editing a source creates a new version.
2. Field of view. Record what a participant was shown before their move. Blind replay hides later work until a contribution is locked or the reader opts to reveal it.
3. Provenance. Tag claims as observed, reported, inferred, or imagined. A fictional outcome and a test result can share a branch and retain different status.
4. Sequence. Keep what happened before and after a move in order. A reaction elicited by our reply belongs to the aftermath and can inform a new appraisal.
5. Scarcity. Show the best alternative that playing this branch would displace. Parking preserves a route without assigning anyone to pursue it. Each parked branch can carry one reason to reopen it.
6. Exit. A participant can pass, write privately, or export what they own or have permission to carry. A public return can summarize an insight without exposing a private source.
7. Inheritance. A new platform publishes its own rules and links to the branch that produced it. Participants can inspect both. Access to source material stays within its original permissions.

