The first board is already in our conversation

Source moment: “Checking platforms.”

• Played branch: Treat it as changing the channel where a social exchange happens.
• Recovered branch: Check whether explaining every fragment is the platform we are stuck on.
• Playable branch: Start with a post that drew a crowd. Move its question to a setting where the people can do something the feed cannot record. Bring back that action and decide what it tells us.

A board formed from the third branch: The Beverage Goblin clip can recruit tasters. The tasting table can observe whether somebody reaches for a second sample when invited. The product team can compare those observations with which flavor and texture people describe. Store recruitment, reactions, and tasting observations separately. Each answers a different question.

Minimal implementation

One invite-only board in a browser. Start with pasted excerpts and Markdown export. Put the source version and permissions on the board, then capture each move and its outcome as separate records.

|Record      |Required fields                                                                                 |
|------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|**Moment**  |ID, source version, excerpt or permitted pointer, visible context, sharing scope                |
|**Move**    |ID, parent moment or move, author, what was visible, proposed action, next-best alternative     |
|**Return**  |Move ID, what was done, what happened, evidence or attribution, sharing scope                   |
|**Platform**|Origin move ID, prompt shown to players, allowed moves, success criterion, exit and export rules|

A move starts as proposed, can be parked or tried, and gains a linked return when someone records an outcome. A separate decision records whether to adopt it. Two moves can come back with conflicting outcomes and stay available for inspection.

Acceptance test. Create one moment with two independent continuations. Reveal them after submission. Turn one into a replay board with its own rules. Attach one observed result and one hypothetical result. Export the board and verify that a new reader can recover the lineage, distinguish the results, and see which context each player had.

Money Bin entry

Classification: Engine candidate with a strategic principle.

Capital it accumulates: Tested ways to recover value from decisions, drafts, and conversations that otherwise discard their alternatives.

First buyers to test: Small creative or product teams that routinely lose useful alternatives across feedback threads and meetings. Sell a private decision or feedback workshop first. Each team brings a real choice, plays independent branches, and leaves with one adopted move, test, or reusable board. Software can follow the repeated parts of that workflow.

Pilot: Run three boards with one willing team across three decisions. Compare each with the team’s normal review process. Record setup time, participant time, and whether a contribution changed the deliverable, decision, or next experiment. At least one independently generated branch must be adopted or tested, and the team must want to bring a second project, before treating the format as a promising product. Track repeats and willingness to pay before estimating revenue.

Failure conditions: If people enjoy writing branches but nobody uses the returns, narrow the board to one decision. If independent plays all repeat the obvious answer, try a different audience or show a different slice of context. If the best alternative costs less to recover in an ordinary conversation, use the conversation and keep the resulting insight.

Promotion rule: Preserve this design as a candidate. Promote the operator when a recovered option produces a useful result that can be traced, inspected, and repeated. The durable asset is the working pattern and its evidence.

